WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.976
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.690
RAJ KANNAN: Welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:09.490
My name is Raj.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.420
I'm with Schlumberger.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.720
I'm the-- got a
fancy title here--

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.530
DELFI Data Ecosystem Manager.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.140
Today, I'll talk about
the petrotechnical suite

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.320
of applications and how we
brought them onto the Google

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.470
Cloud Platform, some of the
challenges that we face,

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.230
and how we worked with
Google to address these.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.340
But before we start, let
me give you an introduction

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.030
to Schlumberger, particularly
for people who might not be

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:40.220
from the oil and gas industry.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:46.420
We are the leader in the
oil and gas services space.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.930
We have about 100,000
or so employees.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:55.000
We are in about 85
different countries.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.770
From the software segment,
which is what I represent,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.280
we've got about 70,000
petrotechnical users

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.150
that are using this.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.260
We have around 1,600 people out
of Schlumberger in the software

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:15.550
segment and about 800 different
individual clients that are

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:19.580
actually using this software.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.752
So when I talk about software,
what do I really mean?

00:01:22.752 --> 00:01:23.710
What are these domains?

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.620
And what problem
spaces are we tackling?

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.310
So if you look at the upstream
exploration and production--

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.040
that's what E&amp;P
here stands for--

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.830
and the challenges
in the space, you

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:40.030
are looking at understanding
of the subsurface,

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:45.280
identifying of the reservoirs,
then on to the execution phase

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.000
in terms of drilling
and completions first

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.120
and then into production
and the operation space.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.070
So our software
suite, as you can

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:59.290
see with the different
branding names down below,

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:04.360
goes across the spectrum, all
the way from the exploration

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.630
side into drilling
and the production,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:11.740
not to forget the economics
aspect of things, right?

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.050
And there are some
infrastructural projects,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.510
which I'm showing in the
last column, that are really

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.970
geared towards how do
we wrangle the data?

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.050
How do we bring the data
into these platforms

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.400
so the users can work
on that, if you will?

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.310
So a few years ago,
the industry sort of

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:36.030
hit a hard spot in terms of the
economic stress, if you will.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:41.070
So the industry's goals really
changed, if you will, right?

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:46.900
So efficiency and the ability
to have cost control or doing

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.970
things in a much more
cost-effective way

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.410
than we had done before
becomes a key aspect for us.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.910
And therefore, what
we were looking

00:02:54.910 --> 00:03:00.070
for is how can we tap into all
of these cloud and big data

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:06.880
technologies, if you will, and
bring them into the oil and gas

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.730
space, particularly
in the upstream E&amp;P,

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.610
and how we can tap
into this, right?

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.360
And traditionally,
where we've been strong

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.170
is the physics aspect
of things, if you will,

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.000
shown by the domain,
both in terms

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.420
of the human services that we
do in terms of data acquisition

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.600
or the execution of the
operations themselves,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.870
but also in terms of
the software that we

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.970
provide to the various groups.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:36.280
So effectively, this was trying
to marry the expertise that we

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.350
have in the domain
and the science

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.590
that we have within our software
and see how can we complement

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.460
this with some of the things
that these cloud technologies

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.620
can bring into this space.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.560
So hence was born DELFI.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.680
So this is something
that we commercialized

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.290
to the marketplace.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:59.620
Last September, we had a
global forum, a similar event

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.290
with our customers in Europe.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.860
And this was released back then.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.110
So like I said,
the key aspect here

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.000
was to try to bring in
aspects around the cloud

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.579
in terms of the scalability, in
terms of tapping into the data

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:19.209
itself, right?

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.459
So there's so many things
that you can do with respect

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:23.270
to the physics side of things.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.200
But when you are trying to drive
efficiency to the next level,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.260
you want to see how much can
we do in terms of automation,

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.820
in terms of taking away the
mundane things, whether it's

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.220
the geoscientist, or
whether this is a reservoir

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.810
engineer or a
production engineer who

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.330
will have to go through on
a day-to-day basis, right?

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.390
So bring the data
science aspect of things

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.180
and supplement that with
the physics that is there

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.530
within our platforms to deliver
more in terms of efficiency

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:53.000
and bootstrapping the users.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.940
So what I'm showing
on the far right

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:58.750
are the key quality attributes
that we were looking for

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.060
and what our customers
were looking for.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.120
Obviously, as we are
tapping into the cloud,

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.440
we need to make sure that
the system is secure.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.040
This is, in fact, particularly
the exploration space,

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.200
a lot of what our
customers are doing

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.290
and how one customer
differentiates themselves

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.130
with the other is in
the way, in the know-how

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:23.240
of how they are processing
the data, how they

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.900
are able to go through an
exploration phase, how they're

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.440
able to identify the reserves
properly, if you will.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.810
So this becomes a key
differentiating factor.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.780
So if data becomes a key
differentiating factor,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.220
we are talking about the cloud.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.730
Then the security aspects
becomes absolutely key.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:43.850
And this is an aspect where
we've spent a lot of time.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.820
So this is one of the
architectural principles

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:51.164
that, to us, is non-negotiable
exactly for this reason, right?

00:05:51.164 --> 00:05:53.080
So everything else in
terms of functionality--

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.450
all the things below that
you see on the list--

00:05:55.450 --> 00:06:00.250
comes after we made sure
that the system is secure.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.490
Datacentric is what I
touched upon in terms

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:06.880
of being able to bring the
data science aspect of things

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.550
and then supplement that
with what our platforms are

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:10.960
able to do.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.230
Openness and extensibility
becomes a key aspect.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:19.220
So in our current
suite of applications,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:22.340
we have a extensibility
platform that we call Ocean.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:26.590
So these are a set
of APIs by which

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.640
our clients, third party--

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.920
in fact, there's a
lot of competitors

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:35.420
who have tapped into it, as
well, to bridge their offerings

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:39.320
into what Schlumberger has been
bringing into the marketplace.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.280
So the same concept has
to extend into the cloud.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.590
We realize that
our customers are

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:48.700
working on things that's way
beyond just the set of tools

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.540
and workflows that we are
going to be providing to them.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:56.590
And the other aspect of the
cloud that we can tap into

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:59.150
is the collaborative and
the personalized aspect.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.860
So unlike other
industries, this is a place

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.760
where you have people that
are working in far-flung areas

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:06.825
out in the field.

00:07:06.825 --> 00:07:08.200
There are people
that are working

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.380
in the offices across
different countries.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:12.840
[AUDIO OUT]

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.950
We are operating in 85
different countries.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.680
So you can imagine the
challenge of distribution

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.780
and collaboration
that is necessary.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:24.160
The expertise typically lives
in the headquarters, right?

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.290
So there are a few experts
that are centralized.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.320
And then the rest of the
teams are distributed.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:33.100
So how do you bring the
right personalized interface

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.530
to get them all to collaborate
and work together as a team?

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.770
So this becomes a key challenge.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.120
And last but not least, a lot
of these things-- and, in fact,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.320
there was an interesting talk
yesterday that I went to.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.640
Arun from Baker
Hughes was talking

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.490
about what they were doing
on the machine learning side.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.470
And he was talking
about uncertainty.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:02.770
So this is a key aspect of
our industry and the challenge

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.660
because you don't get
to see the subsurface.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.120
So there are
certain measurements

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:13.330
that we've done in terms of
well logs or in terms of seismic

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.280
or in some cases core
samples and things like that.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:20.462
So there is little windows
of information that you get.

00:08:20.462 --> 00:08:22.420
And effectively, you're
trying to build a model

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:25.720
to piece together the rest
of the blanks, if you will.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:29.560
So as you start building
these layers of information

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.370
on top of each other, you are
introducing and compounding

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.390
uncertainties as you go through.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.870
So therefore, the
ability to de-risk

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:41.169
this when we are trying
to take decisions

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:43.030
that make a big
economical impact

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.530
becomes really important.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.290
And where we are tapping
into the elasticity

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:50.210
and the scalability of
the cloud is exactly that.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.900
So instead of being
compute limited

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.870
and trying out the
few combinations

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.100
that I can try
out on prem today,

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.950
could I burst this
into the cloud

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:04.510
and make some decisions
that are more accurate,

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.630
bring down the cone of
uncertainty, if you will,

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.757
on the various parameters?

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.410
So I kept talking about
the different domains

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:16.360
and the challenges.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.400
So I wanted to drill down
a little bit more deeper

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.410
and talk about the
different profiles of users

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:22.880
and what types of challenges.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.110
And I'm trying to map this into
the IT or the infrastructure

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:30.710
speech so then we can try to map
it into the cloud technologies

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:32.700
can fit in, if you will.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.410
So let's start off with the
petrophysicist on the top

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.900
and go around in a
loop, if you will.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:41.840
So the petrophysicist
is typically

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.790
working with well data.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:46.760
And given the boon, then
you all have probably

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.440
heard about the
stuff that's going

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:52.770
on in unconventionals and
shale type of workflows.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.820
This now has brought
up a ton of wells.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:00.560
Now you're talking about
hundreds of thousands of wells

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.840
and historical data
that hangs off of this.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.510
So you're talking
about millions of logs

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:08.049
and potentially in the
order of tens of millions,

00:10:08.049 --> 00:10:09.590
if not hundreds of
millions, in terms

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:12.500
of well tops and
properties that are

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.130
associated with it that have
been interpreted by the users.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.690
So the ability to deal with
high-cardinality datasets

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.930
and be effectively acquiring
this, consuming this

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:27.050
from a computation capability
becomes a key challenge

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:28.440
for a petrophysicist.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.290
And today on prem,
we are challenged

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:36.650
by how much you can scale up
the work station for a end user

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.460
or how much more we can farm
out in terms of a local HPC

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:43.680
and its compute capability in
terms of dealing with these,

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.360
right?

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.750
So a lot of the things that I
showed you in the data column

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.700
with our products
were really focused

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:53.610
on the high-cardinality
aspect of things,

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.910
so providing a data
infrastructure that

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.580
backs up what the
applications will need.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.090
Then moving on down into
the reservoir engineer.

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:07.850
So like I said, the way we are
dealing with data in our space

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:10.340
is there's only a few
measurements that we have.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:13.970
The rest is all built
on a digital model

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:15.930
that the users have
interpreted on.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.764
So the reservoir
engineer is a key role,

00:11:18.764 --> 00:11:20.930
where they are trying to
take data that is coming in

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:24.740
from the petrophysicist, the
geologist, the geophysicists,

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.620
blending them all together
to understand what

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.170
the reservoir looks like,
what the reserves in there

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.470
looks like, what the
properties in terms

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.560
of permeability or
fluid flow looks

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:41.390
like, so then we can start to
plan the drilling of a well

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:43.850
into the reservoir and then
find out how effectively we

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:45.350
can get the hydrocarbons.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:49.010
So therefore, the ability
to do the de-risking

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.500
from an uncertainty
perspective and figure out

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.320
how many simulations
can we effectively run

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:58.520
to bring that cone down
becomes really key, OK?

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.640
And then moving on down
in terms of the flow--

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:02.670
assurance engineers.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.444
So these are people that are
in the production domain.

00:12:05.444 --> 00:12:07.610
So you can imagine, once
we have completed the well,

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:10.760
and the well is starting to
produce these hydrocarbons,

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.850
there is a mix of materials
that are coming in.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.090
That will have potentially
an impact on the flow.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:19.127
It will have an impact on the
lifespan of the equipment,

00:12:19.127 --> 00:12:21.710
whether these are the pumps or
whether these are the pipelines

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.300
that we've laid out.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:28.880
So the ability to measure and
monitor the various instruments

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.820
that we have put in, look at
this high-frequency time series

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:36.410
data, and be able to take both
control decisions in near time

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:39.230
and be able to understand the
longer-term impact so we can

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:43.340
then plan for either work overs,
or an alternative strategy

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:45.590
to improve the field
comes into play, right?

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:47.840
So this is where the production
engineers and the flow

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:54.830
assurance engineers come in
and the high velocity of data

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:57.860
that we'll have to deal with
from the various sensors that's

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.520
been instrumented.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.380
So then moving on to the left,
the explorationists-- so these

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:05.580
are typically geologists.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:08.730
But they are looking at it at
a much more abstract scale.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.580
So how does an
oil company decide

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.490
if they need to purchase
a particular field?

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:16.280
How do you know what
the field is worth?

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:19.130
So somebody has to
understand the basin.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.260
They have to understand
the prospects within that

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:23.690
and then define what
a given play is worth.

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.900
So effectively think about
this as the abstract sizing

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:30.260
before you can judge what
the reservoir looks like

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:33.196
and get into the more
technical analysis of this.

00:13:33.196 --> 00:13:34.820
So therefore, these
are people that are

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:37.580
looking at very large areas.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.410
The ability to
visualize the data

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:42.750
in all of these areas, the
ability to scaleably access

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:46.280
the data for this
wide footprint becomes

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.050
really important for them.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:51.140
And that ties into
the geophysicists.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.240
So like I said, the
first piece of data

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.160
that you probably get
into a particular area

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.110
is really the seismic data.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:00.500
And some of the seismic,
particularly in the prestack

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:04.340
world, could be in the order
of terabytes to petabytes.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:06.200
And in a poststack
world, it could

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.360
be in the order of
gigabytes to terabytes.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.870
So how do you effectively
bring them into a space

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.630
where a explorationist
or a geophysicist

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:18.821
can zoom and pan in and
understand the area in a very

00:14:18.821 --> 00:14:19.820
effective manner, right?

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.920
So this is where
they are actually

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.610
interpreting the seismic to
understand what sort of faults

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.610
are in there, what
sort of zones are

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:30.320
in there in terms of
horizons and surfaces

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:32.360
and so on and so forth.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:34.520
And that leads
into the geologist,

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.550
who then has to take such
interpreted data that

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:38.930
is coming in from
the well domain

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.300
from the petrophysicist
and the seismic domain

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:42.440
from the geophysicist.

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:44.850
And then he is
building a model--

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:46.760
an approximation of
the earth so then they

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:53.720
can start to figure out what
the properties of the reservoir

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.230
looks like in this
particular area.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:58.370
So a lot of our
customers are really

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:02.840
looking for a way to go beyond
models into actually natively

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.000
looking at the
data without having

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.570
to have these approximations.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.110
But the models are
a way to come up

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:14.000
with a way to simplify
or wrangle the problem

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.310
into a manageable space.

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:18.620
But even there,
in the past where

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.750
this was in the order
of hundreds of thousands

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:22.580
to million cells,
it's now getting

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.920
into the billions of cells.

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:28.040
And therefore, you can now
imagine simulation type

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.010
of workflows where
the impact of what

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:34.680
is being produced or injected
into one model cell on one time

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:37.280
step impacts the
neighboring cell.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:40.610
So there's a lot of
coupled value extrapolation

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:42.854
that our simulators
will have to do.

00:15:42.854 --> 00:15:44.270
And at that
particular scale, that

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:48.710
becomes a real challenge when it
comes to both the data handling

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:50.940
and the compute capacity.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:54.740
So if I want to shrink all
this into a few bullets

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:58.850
that we can talk about
in the 50 minutes today,

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:02.360
these are the top five things
that I wanted to summarize.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:06.290
So the 3D visualization from
a geophysicist's perspective,

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:09.170
from a geologist's perspective,
from an explorationist's

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:11.840
perspective; the
multiuser collaboration,

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.090
and how do we bring all
of these domains together

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:17.300
so they are all looking
at the data the same way,

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.790
and they're interpreting the
same way, so there isn't a gap

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:23.810
or a misunderstanding between
the different domains?

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:27.260
How do we handle the
large cardinality

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:29.510
of data, particularly
in the well domain,

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:33.630
and the large size of data
in the seismic domain?

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.894
How do we support
concurrent simulations?

00:16:35.894 --> 00:16:37.310
And last but not
the least, like I

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:39.430
said, because these
are global customers,

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.310
they are operating
across the globe.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.770
How do we deploy these services?

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.370
And if we are now offering
this from a cloud perspective,

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:49.310
we need to think about
availability and reliability

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:51.182
and all the SLA aspects
that comes along

00:16:51.182 --> 00:16:52.640
with it, because
they are now going

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:58.230
to rely on our service
for business continuity.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:03.620
So the way we took the
approach in here was obviously,

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.900
we started building
a bunch of workflows

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:09.109
that we are tapping into
the machine learning aspect

00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:10.819
of things and
trying to figure out

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:14.240
how we can provide some
of these interpretation

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:16.490
type of capabilities
using machine learning.

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:18.770
Look for patterns and logs.

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:20.900
Looks for patterns and seismic.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:24.380
And then use that as a
precursor for delivering

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.030
some of the interpretation.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:28.040
But the big challenge
that we ran into

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.340
is if we will now start to
offer some of these capabilities

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:33.320
on the cloud, but
then they are doing

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.020
the other 80% of
the work on prem,

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.960
now there has to be constant
data movement back and forth

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.420
for them to go from start
to finish from a workflow

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:42.750
perspective.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:47.490
So how do we minimize
this impact for the users?

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.750
How do we take the applications
that they are using today,

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:52.709
also bring them
along into the cloud?

00:17:52.709 --> 00:17:54.500
So effectively, you
are shifting the center

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:59.840
of gravity of a asset team or
a business unit of a client

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.040
into the cloud space.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:05.330
So that's where the
petrotechnical suite in DELFI

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:06.630
came into play.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:10.250
So this is effectively
taking our applications

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.950
that typically run on desktops.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:15.180
Most of these are
windows applications.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:17.409
We do have a few
Linux applications.

00:18:17.409 --> 00:18:19.700
So this is where the user
interfaces, and this is where

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:21.410
the users are interfacing.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:25.520
And then those rely on two types
of capabilities on the back.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:29.150
One is around the data in
terms of app servers, database

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.230
servers, and so on and so forth.

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:34.760
And the other one is in terms of
the actual physics that's built

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:37.310
into our numerical simulators.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:39.440
And that's what is
classified as engines.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:43.130
So by bringing these into the
Google Cloud Platform, now

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:44.930
the user has one place to go.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:46.160
The data is in one place.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:50.450
So whether they are tapping
into some of these native DELFI

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:53.210
workflows and the native
capabilities in terms

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:54.980
of machine learning
and automation

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:58.220
that we are bringing in,
or in terms of continuing

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.370
their workflow in the
platforms or the engines

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:05.280
that they are used to, it's all
in the same environment, right?

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.150
So that was the
driver for why we

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:11.930
commercialize the
petrotechnical suite in DELFI.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.710
So what does that look like?

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:17.520
So let's take the journey
of Patrick, for example.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.120
So the first thing
that we figure out

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:21.950
is, OK, what is his profile?

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:24.500
So if he's a
reservoir engineer, he

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:26.420
logs in into the DELFI portal.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:32.660
So this is effectively a
bunch of app engine services

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:36.410
and Kubernetes clusters
that are running in GKE.

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:41.529
And this is where we manage the
overall interface to the users,

00:19:41.529 --> 00:19:42.320
if you will, right?

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.250
So from a user
experience perspective,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:48.100
how we manage the subscriptions,
understand which client

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.780
that they belong to, what
data sets they have access to,

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:52.172
and so on and so forth.

00:19:52.172 --> 00:19:53.630
And this is also
the place where we

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:56.240
have linked some of
the soft services,

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:58.580
meaning the human
services, whether this is

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:01.880
support, health, consultancy.

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:04.010
They want to acquire
new data, in which case

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:06.320
we can have our sister
segments come in

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:08.580
and enable them with
data acquisition

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:09.960
and so and so forth.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:11.630
And effectively,
what the portal does

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:15.320
is after it has figured out
who the user is and understands

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.840
that in this particular case,
he's a reservoir engineer,

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.600
routes him to a
production environment

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.510
that you see on the
right side, where

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:24.440
we've got all of
his applications

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:27.260
and all of his data
storage, right?

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.900
And we know that
over a period of time

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:32.400
we need to continue to
update these applications.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.100
It's not a one-time shot.

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:36.920
So therefore, from a
continuous integration,

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.900
continuous delivery
perspective, we also

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:40.760
create a lab environment.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:44.600
So when there is a newer version
of these products that we try

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:48.110
out, if the customer has
additional extensions

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:50.750
or plug-ins and things
like that they have built,

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.880
they have one cohesive
environment where they can

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.300
actually test and validate the
full length doing workflow--

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:59.780
Schlumberger and
non-Schlumberger services

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:00.710
together--

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:03.750
and then qualify that to be
promoted into the production

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:04.670
environment.

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:07.400
The final visualization
of the applications

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:10.160
and how they consume
it, if you will--

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.830
the native workflows are
all just web interfaces.

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:16.220
And so effectively, it's
ubiquitous access to a browser.

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:19.400
For some of these desktop
applications that are really

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:22.400
relying on the
visualization aspects,

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:23.880
we use thin client
visualizations--

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:25.880
so effectively doing
server-side rendering

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:30.230
and then transmitting the
results back into the browser,

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:31.500
if you will.

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:35.180
So again, taking those
same five key challenges

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:38.630
and then zooming it down
into the petrotechnical suite

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.450
potion of DELFI, let's jump
into some of the details

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:44.760
and how we went
through the challenges.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:49.040
So the first one is around
the 3D visualization, right?

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:52.550
This one is a key problem
for us because if you

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:54.770
look at how most
of our users work,

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:57.822
they have probably
dual 4K monitors.

00:21:57.822 --> 00:21:59.780
This is where they are
sitting and interpreting

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:01.100
eight hours a day.

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:04.520
If you have now taken an
intensive application like this

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:06.650
and moved that
into the cloud, we

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:10.160
need to make sure that it has
the same level of performance

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:13.490
that they are used to
today on prem, right?

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:17.840
So just to give you an analog
to how we may watch a Netflix

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:21.080
movie, for example, you may
be sitting on a 4K screen

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:22.070
and watching a movie.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.680
So it's a one-way
stream of data that

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:26.540
is coming in from the servers.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.270
In this particular case,
it's actually a two-way.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:32.810
And it's more than
one 4K screen, right?

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:34.640
Because every time
the user decides

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:38.120
to move, pan, interpret,
click, the application

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.040
has to react as if
the application was

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.800
running on a box that's
right next to him, right?

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:47.420
So the ability to work
around the challenges

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:49.500
that the internet
brings into this space,

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:53.210
right-- so whether this is
things like packet loss, what's

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:54.350
the bandwidth.

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:57.050
A lot of the customers,
and particularly some

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:59.140
of the locations where
they are operating,

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:02.140
there's no guarantees in terms
of the right level of bandwidth

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:03.320
that they may have.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.620
So we need to make
sure how optimally we

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:09.610
can design the system
so we can deliver

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.820
the right level of experience
with the minimum overhead

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:14.830
in terms of the
bandwidth requirement.

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.780
And likewise, we also
have to think about

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:19.060
from a global
deployment perspective,

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:22.480
how do we put these
applications close enough, so

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.700
that way the overall latency--

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:27.220
I mean, assume that
the visualization

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:28.990
aspect, the
computation aspect has

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:32.260
no penalty if the
customer is, let's say,

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:34.150
200 milliseconds away?

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:37.900
So by the time a frame comes up,
and the customer clicks on it,

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.690
and the application reacts to
it, you're looking at a half

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:42.610
a second, right?

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:43.990
And it may not seem much.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:47.500
But if that's what you work on
8 hours a day, 40 hours a week,

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.380
pretty soon that becomes
completely unusable, right?

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:54.190
So therefore, the ability to
understand where the user is

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:57.820
and bring the application
closer to where the user is,

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:01.570
so we can bring it within
a reasonable latency,

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:05.690
becomes a key deployment and
operational challenge as well.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:07.660
So from a journey
perspective, where we started

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:10.515
was really on the K80s.

00:24:10.515 --> 00:24:11.890
Pretty soon we
realized that this

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.470
was more geared towards
the computer rather than

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:16.170
the visualization.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:20.500
And where we are now
is with the P100 GPUs.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:24.010
And I will talk about the
different areas or the regions

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.040
where we have the
service deployed today.

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:30.880
And where we'll be
moving to is towards P4.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:33.375
And this has been a fantastic
journey with Google,

00:24:33.375 --> 00:24:36.100
where they've been
constantly helping us

00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:38.390
through this journey.

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:41.950
There are a few issues
in terms of where

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:45.970
we are with P100s, because
P100s, even though technically

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:49.960
supports vGPU, is also
built more for compute

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.520
rather than for visualization.

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.740
So therefore, there
are still challenges

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:57.400
in terms of having to bring
our own in media grid licenses.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:01.000
And when the machines, from
a Google infrastructure

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:02.860
perspective, go
through a migration,

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.080
even the machines
have to shut down.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.450
So imagine if you are a user.

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:09.400
You are remotely accessing
one of these applications.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:11.710
And a live migration
event hits, right?

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.370
So that becomes a workflow
issue that we are now

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:18.740
working with Google to resolve
this with the new P4 offering

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:19.990
that they are bringing online.

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:29.100
So this is a video that
I shot on my laptop.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:32.070
And effectively, what
you are seeing here

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:38.890
is our application Petrel that
is being served out of Iowa,

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:39.900
in this particular case.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:41.370
And I'm based in Houston.

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:44.370
So I've just shot it
in my laptop screen.

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.710
So it's a 4K screen
that's being served

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.290
across the continental US.

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:54.780
And we are getting anywhere
between 20 to about 30,

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:56.420
35 frames per second.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:26:00.690
And anything about 20, then
the user's perception that this

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:01.990
is actually remote goes away.

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:06.500
It feels much more
local and immersive.

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:08.000
So the other
challenge, like I said,

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.940
was user are in
different locations.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:12.960
How do we bring them together?

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:15.120
How can they work in
the space together?

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:18.500
So here I am showing
three different profiles

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.310
that are working together.

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:23.180
And in this particular
case, the client environment

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:26.420
was running out of
the South Carolina

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:30.380
region for Google from where we
were serving the applications

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:32.520
to these different users.

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:34.970
So this brings up a
number of challenges,

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:38.730
particularly starting with
the security side of things.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:41.720
So how do we, out of all of
these users on the internet,

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.790
identify these users?

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:45.290
And how do we
identify that these

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:48.600
are the users that also
belong to the same tenant,

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:50.990
if you will, and
therefore can share data

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:53.450
and can collaborate
effectively together?

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.960
So the step one in that
was the authentication

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.970
and the authorization process.

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:02.090
So we spent a lot of time
with the Firebase team

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.390
and the Google
team looking at how

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:08.600
we can tap into Google's IAM.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:15.050
So effectively, our users are
mirrored into Google's IdP.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:16.670
There are a few
customers, including

00:27:16.670 --> 00:27:18.440
Schlumberger's own
internal use, where

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:22.190
we have federated with Google
and where we haven't federated

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:24.860
with Google, we go through
a registration in DELFI,

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:28.250
which effectively creates the
users in the Google space.

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:32.600
That now allows us to do
a basic authentication

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:35.720
and get all the benefits
of Google's ability

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:38.660
to look for anomalies
and to validate

00:27:38.660 --> 00:27:40.310
that the authentication
truly comes

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:43.250
from the user and the location
and the device from where they

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:45.834
are trusted to access
the applications.

00:27:45.834 --> 00:27:48.500
And then on the back end of it--
and this is where the challenge

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.324
for the petrotechnical
suite comes in--

00:27:50.324 --> 00:27:51.740
is these are
applications that are

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:54.390
running on Windows and Linux.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:58.040
So they don't speak a
G-token or an Auth 2 token.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:27:59.540
So effectively,
we'll have to bridge

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:02.120
the gap between a
internet-friendly

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:05.180
authentication token and what
the desktop applications truly

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:06.450
need.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:09.920
So what we do is we have
a service called SAuth,

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:11.550
or Schlumberger authentication.

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:14.550
Think of this as a
handshake service

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:18.630
that once Google has validated
that this user is indeed

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:21.840
Patrick from Cardinal Oil,
we can then turn around

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:25.380
and enable an account and
launch the application

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:27.144
under the user's account.

00:28:27.144 --> 00:28:28.560
And that's what
you see on the far

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:33.230
right by this thing that we are
calling the Tenant AD system.

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:36.480
So that shadow identity is what
we use, so these applications

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:39.540
don't have to worry about
cloud-based identities.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:42.560
They can still happily work
in the traditional Windows

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.580
and Linux space, if you will.

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:47.415
So here's a video.

00:28:47.415 --> 00:28:49.540
Sorry about the fact that
it's a little bit grainy.

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:53.410
I think once we imported it,
I lost some resolution here.

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:56.470
But what I'm showing
here is two users.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:29:01.920
So the user [AUDIO OUT]
has two 4K monitors.

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:06.090
And I also opened up
my laptop in there.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.370
So you can see three screens--

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:10.680
two 4K, one HD screen.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.930
And then the laptop
in the front is

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.580
designed to illustrate
to you a user who

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:20.490
is remote, that is looking
at the same screen as what

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:21.750
the primary user is.

00:29:21.750 --> 00:29:23.880
So back to the
collaboration workflow,

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:27.150
just like we are taking the
output of the application

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.550
and presenting it to
the user in Houston,

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:31.830
we can present the same
application interface

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:34.780
to a user that is
sitting in California.

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:38.822
So now this effectively creates
a virtual room, if you will,

00:29:38.822 --> 00:29:41.280
where these people are looking
at the cameras the same way.

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:44.070
They're looking at the
applications the same way.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:46.350
And just as an analog,
if you compare this

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:51.750
to a Skype or a Webex or a
linked type of remote sharing,

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.900
these are not really
built for the kind

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.570
of 3D acceleration
and the interactivity

00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:59.820
that you need to
be able to foster

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:01.320
this kind of a
collaboration, right?

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:04.720
So this is the high
end of the spectrum.

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.790
And that's what that looks like.

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:09.240
So then jumping onto
the aspect number

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:13.620
three, in terms of the data
sets and the challenges there,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:17.250
like I said, there is two
real extreme categories,

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:17.980
if you will.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:20.920
One is on the large data sets.

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:25.230
So this could be in the case
of seismic terabytes of data

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:28.120
that we may have to bring
into an application space.

00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:32.520
So what is the overall IOPS that
my storage system can support

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:36.150
to be able to deliver that
volume of data, particularly

00:30:36.150 --> 00:30:40.300
in a random I/O pattern
into the applications?

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:42.750
And then off on the other
end of the spectrum,

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:45.185
remember the petrophysicist
and geologist type of workflow

00:30:45.185 --> 00:30:46.560
that I was
mentioning, where they

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:49.560
are dealing with
millions of data

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:52.380
items in the wellbore domain.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:54.330
And these are
really small pieces

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:57.600
of data but higher
cardinality of data.

00:30:57.600 --> 00:31:00.930
So I'm sure you all have
had the experience where

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:04.500
if you throw, let's say, 10,000
files into a file system,

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.960
you can see how much
it slows down, right?

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:13.380
So the ability for file
systems and their ability

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:17.850
to handle both
large-file random I/O

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:20.520
and small and high
cardinality of files,

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:24.270
and how you deal with this
from a directory and a delivery

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:26.860
perspective stresses
out the system, right?

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:30.830
So today, what most of
our customers are using

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:33.810
are these really high-end,
purpose-built systems

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:39.310
from the likes of EMC or
NetApp and so on and so forth.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:43.210
So now imagine trying to
bring this into the cloud.

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:45.220
So some of our new
solutions, they

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:49.000
have the advantage of tapping
into Google's elasticity

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:52.250
in terms of things like Google
Cloud Storage, and BigQuery,

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:53.750
and so on and so forth.

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:56.110
But what do I do about
applications that still

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:58.990
rely on a service file system?

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:01.810
How do you bring the same
level of performance?

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:06.010
And these are several hundreds
of man-years worth of effort

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:07.810
put in into these
applications to bake

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:10.000
the physics and the
user experience into it.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.380
So it's not possible
to overnight

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:13.690
just revamp the storage
and then switch it

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:17.466
over to use a BigQuery or
a cloud storage, right?

00:32:17.466 --> 00:32:19.090
So it's going to be
a journey, so which

00:32:19.090 --> 00:32:22.240
means we need to solve this
problem in the interim.

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:25.420
So we looked at a number
of both open source

00:32:25.420 --> 00:32:27.537
and commercial solutions.

00:32:27.537 --> 00:32:29.620
Some of the things that I
haven't listed here-- we

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:31.440
looked at things like Aware.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:34.150
We looked at NetApp.

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:38.260
The big challenge that we
had was getting the level

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:40.000
of performance that we need.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.610
So particularly in
the seismic space

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:46.210
for the sort of visualizations
that you were seeing there,

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:50.710
we need close to 400 MBPS
in terms of throughput.

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:52.540
And it's not a single
channel throughput

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:55.180
because this is multiple
users that are concurrently

00:32:55.180 --> 00:32:56.330
running these applications.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.060
So you need multiple channels
of throughput at the same time.

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:01.340
So how do we go
about doing that?

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:05.740
So we did a number
of tests with Google.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.240
And Red Hat was
the other partner

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.700
that was involved in helping
sort out the implementation,

00:33:10.700 --> 00:33:12.040
if you will.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.780
In fact, this was one
of those components

00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:17.320
that we rewrote and rewrote
over and over again just

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:19.330
to fine tune it to
the right aspect.

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:22.060
So we ended up with ZFS.

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:25.750
This is a old
component from Solaris.

00:33:25.750 --> 00:33:27.700
But in combination
with Google, we

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:33.610
were able to tune it to provide
the right level of storage

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:35.050
back end, if you will.

00:33:35.050 --> 00:33:38.680
And the other aspect
is the memory.

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:41.680
So this is probably even
pushing Google to the limits

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:45.700
in terms of what a GCE compute
VM's infrastructure needs

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:46.730
to look like.

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:49.870
So as you can see, when you're
talking about remote graphics

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:54.160
and dealing with a larger
volume of data visualization,

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:57.070
we need a high-end GPU
to be able to do that.

00:33:57.070 --> 00:34:01.240
To bring enough data into memory
and be able to visualize this,

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:05.170
we need a machine that can
give that user workstation

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:06.800
a ton of memory space.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:10.060
So most of our users
typically have 1/2 a terabyte

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:13.610
or 3/4 of a terabyte on
their workstations today.

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:15.370
So how do we get
the amount of memory

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:19.000
that is necessary to
hold this data up and do

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:20.469
the visualization?

00:34:20.469 --> 00:34:22.491
And then on the back end
of it comes the storage

00:34:22.491 --> 00:34:23.949
and the throughput
that the storage

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:25.560
has to deliver into this.

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:31.310
So where we are-- and if you
see the picture on the top left,

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:36.889
we are maxing out the spectrum
of where we could be today.

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:40.540
So for a GPU-based
machine, today we

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:43.319
can go up to 208 gigs of RAM.

00:34:43.319 --> 00:34:44.860
And again, we are
working with Google

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:47.230
to push that limit
even higher, getting

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:49.389
it closer to where
we need to be,

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:52.030
which is in the 1/2 a
terabyte range, right?

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:53.290
So today, it's a dichotomy.

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:56.949
You can get the high-end
machines in GCE,

00:34:56.949 --> 00:34:58.150
but you don't get the GPUs.

00:34:58.150 --> 00:35:03.100
So we need the GPUs and the
high-end machines, if you will.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.230
And the other collaboration
that we have started on

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:08.120
is the Cloud File Store.

00:35:08.120 --> 00:35:10.550
So this is a new service
that's coming in from Google.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:13.390
So we don't have to be
bothered with the deployment

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:15.970
and the management and the
elasticity of the storage

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:18.520
solution that we've
put together ourselves.

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:22.940
So jumping into the
simulators, so like I said,

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:26.870
there is a bunch of physics that
we have baked into simulators,

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:29.230
whether this is in the
petroleum systems space,

00:35:29.230 --> 00:35:31.640
whether this is in the
economic space, flow

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:35.740
assurance, mechanics,
reservoir engineering.

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:38.740
So we have simulators purpose
built for each of the domains

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:43.330
and the kind of challenges
that those domains have.

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:48.100
And what we have done with
the DELFI offering on Google

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:51.310
is we are now offering that
cloud-based dynamic compute.

00:35:51.310 --> 00:35:54.410
And what does that really mean?

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.730
Now, I don't have to
be worried about a job

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:00.550
queue and a fixed
HPC cluster size,

00:36:00.550 --> 00:36:03.850
and how do we get a bunch
of reservoir engineers

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:06.850
to fit their workloads in here.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:09.661
If a drilling rig is
becoming available,

00:36:09.661 --> 00:36:12.160
and I need to make a decision
within the next couple of days

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:14.240
whether I want to
employ this or not,

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:16.480
I can go ahead and
spend the nodes that

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:21.250
is necessary not only to do
that once simulation but also

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:21.910
many, right?

00:36:21.910 --> 00:36:24.910
So here's a illustration of what
a reservoir simulation looks

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:25.640
like.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:28.720
So these are the model
cells that you see here.

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:31.150
And the two blue
sticks that you see

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.460
onto the left are the injectors.

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:35.650
And you can see that
as the simulation runs,

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:38.260
you can start to
see how the water is

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:42.780
pushing the hydrocarbons
out through the other wells,

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:43.840
if you will.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:46.350
So this is one
simulation and how

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:48.780
you can go through it in time.

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:53.640
But what we are now able to do
with the Google infrastructure

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.760
is you can take that one
aspect of it and say,

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.800
what are my uncertainties?

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:02.970
What happens if the permeability
that I thought was 0.7

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:07.191
is actually between
0.65 and 0.75?

00:37:07.191 --> 00:37:10.470
Could I run a whole bunch
of these simulations

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:13.980
for the variability in
my uncertainty spectrum

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:17.621
and understand what my P10
and my P50, P90 use cases

00:37:17.621 --> 00:37:18.120
look like?

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:20.430
And therefore, I
can start to de-risk

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:25.260
my estimate of the reservoir
and the flow in a much more

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:26.980
effective manner.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:30.720
So obviously, this gives
us additional opportunities

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:32.290
to optimize here.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:35.040
So what I'm showing you here
with respect to the graph

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:38.910
is the white area of
the graph are basically

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:42.600
the nodes that are active
at any given point in time.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:44.290
Anything that is
blue are the nodes

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:48.060
that are either being warmed
up or being passivated.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:52.920
And the orange ones are
the ones that are idle.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:55.230
And if you can't see the
blue and the orange ones,

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.150
that means we have
done a fairly good job.

00:37:57.150 --> 00:37:59.580
And that is the
point here, that when

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.390
it comes to the elasticity,
we don't want to have computes

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:05.370
sitting there idle, right?

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:09.690
And the y-axis here is in the
order of tens of thousands

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:10.614
of nodes.

00:38:10.614 --> 00:38:12.030
And you can see
how effectively we

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.760
are using the Google
infrastructure to map

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:17.250
to what the customer's load is.

00:38:17.250 --> 00:38:20.970
So this brings
both the elasticity

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:23.970
that they want to de-risk and
deal with the uncertainties

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:25.470
that they are dealing
with, and also

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:27.830
bring the cost-efficiency
on the other side

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:31.300
so that you're not ending
up paying for a HPC

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:33.480
cluster for peak
capacity and having

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:37.840
to deal with the cost aspects
that are associated with it.

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:42.510
So that said, there are some
challenges in this space

00:38:42.510 --> 00:38:43.300
as well.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:47.500
So today, customers
have purpose-built HPCs

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:49.170
set up on prem.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:52.260
And these are running
with an InfiniBand

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:54.000
back plain, if you will.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:57.600
So effectively, because these
are MPI-based applications,

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:00.360
and they have to share
data for each time step

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:02.670
across these cells, there's
a lot of communication

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:04.020
that goes along, right?

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:07.230
So we've tried to optimize
this by trying to do a hybrid.

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.410
So where we can do shared
memory in cases where

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:14.430
the compute instances can be
co-located in the same node,

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:17.190
and in other cases, we fall
back to network communication

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:19.170
over MPI.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:23.370
And Chris actually pointed
me to the multi-ping latency

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:25.374
that I'm showing you
here, because just

00:39:25.374 --> 00:39:26.790
like everything
else in the cloud,

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:28.150
things are constantly changing.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:30.370
So by the time you
publish a metric,

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:32.190
the metric is actually old.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:36.390
So the numbers have
come down on GCE.

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:40.590
As you see in the chart here,
it's a little bit dated.

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:42.450
So the other one
that we did was we

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:45.760
compared this with
other cloud providers.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:48.000
We compared this with
various configurations

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:49.890
that we can do on Google.

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:51.330
There were a couple
of features--

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.860
and we like to think that
Google did this for us.

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:58.530
But I think it's of general
benefit to the community.

00:39:58.530 --> 00:40:01.680
So one is around sole tenancy.

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:06.240
So this ensures that we can
have a set of nodes that

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:09.190
are assigned to work together.

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:11.610
So therefore, we can
bring down the latency

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:14.820
of the internode communication
for nodes that are part

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:17.070
of that sole tenancy scope.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:21.300
They also introduced the
direct word I/O, which also

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:23.430
shaves the latency quite a bit.

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:26.520
And more recently, with
the Skylake processors

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:32.480
and its effective use of the
cache and vNUMA, if you will,

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:33.940
we've effectively
brought it down.

00:40:33.940 --> 00:40:35.860
So if you look at the
chart on the right side,

00:40:35.860 --> 00:40:37.318
so this is the
performance variance

00:40:37.318 --> 00:40:39.990
that I'm showing across the
different options, the very

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.470
first column being what
the really high-end

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:47.550
HPCs that the customers have on
prem, versus where we ended up

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:51.540
with the Google Compute
Engine, with the combination

00:40:51.540 --> 00:40:54.880
of direct word I/O, Skylake,
and sole tenancy, right?

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:59.370
So we are extremely close
in terms of the high end

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:03.270
without having that huge cost
of deployment and maintenance

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:06.330
and whatnot, right?

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:08.610
So last but not the
least, so so far,

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:10.640
we looked at some of the
technical challenges.

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:13.770
Now, let's look at the
operational challenges.

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:19.350
And first off, a shout-out
to the SRE team in Google.

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:22.380
They spent a lot of
time with us trying

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:26.100
to help us define where we want
to deploy these services, how

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:30.000
we want to instrument these
services from a monitoring

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.850
perspective, how do we
define the right level

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:36.570
of service-level indicators
and service-level objectives,

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:39.540
figuring out the error budgets
so our operations team can

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:41.790
be more efficient,
and figuring out

00:41:41.790 --> 00:41:43.950
what are the key problems
that we need to focus

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:46.050
on operationally,
rather than going

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:48.600
after every problem that's
going to pop up, right?

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:51.240
So today, from a
GPU perspective,

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:55.030
we are in South Carolina,
Belgium, and Taiwan.

00:41:55.030 --> 00:41:58.980
We are working with Google to
expand this to other centers.

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:02.130
And the other regions that
are out there we can support

00:42:02.130 --> 00:42:03.900
are 2D applications.

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:06.780
But we're waiting on the
GPUs for the 3D applications,

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:08.370
if you will.

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:10.570
So a few other things--

00:42:10.570 --> 00:42:15.930
so this was a necessary
evil for us to solve.

00:42:15.930 --> 00:42:19.770
I mean, if we went with a Google
Cloud Storage or a BigQuery

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:21.960
or whatever, the
advantage is Google

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:24.270
takes care of the
disaster recovery.

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:27.180
But when you overlay
a shared file system--

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:28.980
in this particular
case, a ZFS file

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:32.850
system-- across multiple nodes
and multiple persistent disks

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:35.790
effectively, the metadata-- so
the individual persistent disks

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:37.500
are taken care of by Google.

00:42:37.500 --> 00:42:39.450
But the overall
shared storage setup

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:40.960
and the metadata
that goes with it,

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:42.390
we will have to take care of it.

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:44.790
And the good thing
was the ability

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:48.000
to have snapshots on the
persistent disks is something

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:49.870
that came with Google for free.

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:52.960
And it's very effective in terms
of the incremental snapshot

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:56.970
that it overlays and how
quickly we can restore in case

00:42:56.970 --> 00:43:01.140
there was ever a fault.

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:04.260
So the other thing that we
did was around consistency

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:05.740
and reliability.

00:43:05.740 --> 00:43:08.460
So this was a very
important learning for us.

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:11.550
On prem, we've gotten
used to the fact

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:15.930
that machines do not go down.

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:16.919
Disks don't fail.

00:43:16.919 --> 00:43:18.960
There used to be a time
when blue screen of death

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:21.660
was your day-to-day reality.

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:23.380
But that's not the
case anymore, right?

00:43:23.380 --> 00:43:26.430
So systems have become
much more reliable.

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:30.960
And therefore, the determinism
of the availability

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:33.810
or the performance
of a system has come

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:36.307
to the user as a given, right?

00:43:36.307 --> 00:43:37.890
But when you're
dealing with the cloud

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:39.890
and with the number of
things that are moving,

00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:42.720
your virtual stack is way deep.

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:45.870
We have to deal with the
fact that fault tolerance is

00:43:45.870 --> 00:43:48.810
something that we'll have
to build into the systems.

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:50.960
Be able to switch over
into a different node

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:54.600
if, for example, a live
migration event kicks in.

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:57.510
And the ability for
the infrastructure

00:43:57.510 --> 00:44:00.120
to allocate a
resource to you also

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:02.654
depends on the load on
the particular system.

00:44:02.654 --> 00:44:04.320
So what I'm showing
here with the charts

00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:07.480
is we started monitoring
what the variance looks like.

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:10.170
So if I ask for a VM to
be created, user comes in,

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:11.520
starts an application.

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:14.550
How long before the
application shows up for him?

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:16.830
And the more deterministic,
the less variance

00:44:16.830 --> 00:44:19.740
we can bring into the system,
the better the user experience

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:20.770
is.

00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:23.790
So this was a lot of work
that we did with the GCE team

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:27.870
in Google to bring down
that variance that you

00:44:27.870 --> 00:44:32.590
see on the various aspects.

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:37.050
And this is around the
availability of the system

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:39.600
and being able to
continuously monitor this.

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:44.280
So that way, we can use
our few precious SREs

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:49.860
and operational teams to focus
on management by exception,

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:52.590
rather than having to
babysit these systems, right?

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:56.250
So we leverage
Stackdriver quite a bit.

00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:59.550
So we monitor both the
metrics that are coming in,

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:01.710
and we also route all
of the event logs,

00:45:01.710 --> 00:45:04.990
including the application
logs, into the system.

00:45:04.990 --> 00:45:09.180
So in the past, if there
was a crash or a performance

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:11.190
impact on one of
our applications,

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:13.140
the customer has to
call us and let us know,

00:45:13.140 --> 00:45:14.320
this is what happened.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:16.434
And we have to ask
them, OK, what data set?

00:45:16.434 --> 00:45:17.100
What did you do?

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:18.750
Why did this problem happen?

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:20.250
And now, effectively
what we've done

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:22.470
is we've flipped
support on its head.

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:24.640
Instead of support
being reactive,

00:45:24.640 --> 00:45:27.540
it's now becoming
proactive, all right?

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:30.150
So by looking at
these logs, these

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:33.540
are now creating events
that trigger an alert

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:35.470
to the operations team.

00:45:35.470 --> 00:45:37.590
So these days, by the
time the customer calls,

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:39.840
they are pleasantly
surprised that the operations

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:42.882
team is already aware of what's
the issue that's going on.

00:45:42.882 --> 00:45:44.340
And they're actually
working on it.

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:45.750
So by the time
they call, they're

00:45:45.750 --> 00:45:49.230
actually getting an estimate of
when it's going to be addressed

00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:51.870
or what a workaround
is that will restore

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:55.260
business continuity for them.

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:58.050
A few more things
on the monitoring.

00:45:58.050 --> 00:46:01.680
So this is application
usage and security.

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:04.350
So beyond what
Google does in terms

00:46:04.350 --> 00:46:07.260
of protecting the cloud
and the infrastructure,

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:09.900
we do realize that we
have our own applications

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:11.130
and our own end points.

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:13.620
And there is extensibility
on these end points.

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:15.370
So what you see on the
chart on the right,

00:46:15.370 --> 00:46:18.460
for example, is monitoring
all the failed logins.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:21.960
So typically, it's very easy
to think about the things

00:46:21.960 --> 00:46:25.230
that you want to monitor,
which is the good-day scenario,

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:26.190
if you will.

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:28.260
But we also have to
be very cognizant

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:30.660
when it comes to security
on the cloud to take

00:46:30.660 --> 00:46:33.400
care of these edge cases.

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:37.680
So anyway, to summarize,
this is a journey

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:39.840
that we went
through with Google.

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:42.030
We talked about
the five challenges

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:46.260
and how we are effectively
leveraging both Google's

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:49.560
expertise in this area and the
Google Cloud infrastructure

00:46:49.560 --> 00:46:51.060
to tackle these.

00:46:51.060 --> 00:46:56.100
And this is a solution that's
been commercial for almost

00:46:56.100 --> 00:46:57.300
a year now.

00:46:57.300 --> 00:47:00.120
And even though I have only
covered a small portion

00:47:00.120 --> 00:47:02.190
of the challenges,
hopefully it gives you

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:05.490
a flavor for what it takes
in the oil and gas space

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:08.340
to bring in our software
suite and bring it

00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:10.950
into a cloud environment.

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:13.920
But on the positive side,
there is a lot of advantages

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:16.920
that our users have got
on the system already.

00:47:16.920 --> 00:47:19.950
So we talked about the reservoir
simulation and the benefits

00:47:19.950 --> 00:47:20.850
so far.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:22.710
But you can see, both
on the petrophysics

00:47:22.710 --> 00:47:25.050
and on the seismic
space, not only

00:47:25.050 --> 00:47:27.420
with the petrotechnical
suite but also

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:29.560
with the other
analytical solutions

00:47:29.560 --> 00:47:31.310
that we are bringing
in to supplement what

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:33.510
the petrotechnical
suite does, we

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:37.050
were able to greatly
improve the productivity.

00:47:37.050 --> 00:47:40.920
And because Schlumberger
is also a big-time user

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:44.580
of our own software
for seismic processing,

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:47.010
consulting, data
delivery, et cetera,

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:50.771
we've also improved the way
we deliver our own services,

00:47:50.771 --> 00:47:51.270
right?

00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:54.870
So these are some of the
stats in terms of the benefits

00:47:54.870 --> 00:47:57.940
that the Google and Schlumberger
partnership has brought

00:47:57.940 --> 00:48:00.240
into the oil and gas industry.

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:02.880
And I will close tying
it back to the first way

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:07.080
that we started, with
the economical pressures

00:48:07.080 --> 00:48:10.510
that the industry
is facing today.

00:48:10.510 --> 00:48:14.190
The solution that we have put in
here with respect to automation

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:16.410
and bringing the
efficiency is greatly

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:18.370
appreciated by
the market, right.

00:48:18.370 --> 00:48:20.359
So I think I'm
fairly out of time.

00:48:20.359 --> 00:48:22.650
But if there's any questions,
I can either take it now,

00:48:22.650 --> 00:48:24.040
or we can take it offline.

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:27.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

